\\( k \\) is also 90 degrees. This means the angle adjacent to \\( x \\) (let's call it \\(\\angle 3\\)) is 90 degrees.\n\n3. **Use the straight line property:**\n   - The angles on a straight line sum up to 180 degrees. Therefore, the angle adjacent to \\( x \\) and the 30-degree angle form a straight line.\n   - So, \\(\\angle 3 = 180^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 150^\\circ\\).\n\n4. **Find the measure of angle \\( x \\):**\n   - Since \\(\\angle 3\\) and \\( x \\) are supplementary angles (they add up to 180 degrees), we have:\n     \\[\n     x + 90^\\circ = 150^\\circ\n     \\]\n   - Solving for \\( x \\):\n     \\[\n     x = 150^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{60}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{60}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:124"}
